    correctAnswerText: "atrial ECG",
    explanation: "Atrial ECG allows direct recording of the atrial electrical impulses and therefore clearly delineates the presence and timing of p waves relative to the QRS complex. A standard 12-lead ECG is acceptable, but may not allow clear differentiation between sinus tachycardia and junctional ectopic tachycardia. Adenosine administration is the treatment of choice for supraventricular tachycardia and is not diagnostic without continuous ECG monitoring. Echocardiography is not indicated in this situation. Overdrive pacing may be used to improve atrioventricular synchrony in junctional ectopic tachycardia, but it is a therapeutic measur",
