    scenario: "A 2-year-old boy presents to the pediatric ICU following cardiac surgery with a previous cardiac history of mitral atresia status post previous Blalock-Taussig shunt and bidirectional Glenn procedure. Pre-Fontan cardiac catheterization showed a dilated right ventricle and a pulmonary vascular resistance of 5 Woods units. Today, the patient underwent an extracardiac nonfenestrated Fontan procedure and presents to the pediatric ICU mechanically ventilated on a milrinone infusion. The patient is quickly weaned from the ventilator and extubated. Vital signs are pulse 142, respiratory rate 22, blood pressure 82/46 mmHg, and oxygen saturation 93% on 60% FiO2 and inhaled nitric oxide. Left atrial pressure is 6 mmHg, and central venous pressure (CVP) is 20 mmHg. The patient requires multiple boluses of fluid over the initial 6 hours post-op for hypotension. The patient has responded poorly to the fluid boluses and has a blood pressure of 62/33 mm Hg with a heart rate of 152 bpm. The chest tubes have minimal output. The patient’s liver is 7 cm below the costal margin. Two hours ago, the pediatric ICU fellow had started an epinephrine infusion at 0.05 µg/kg/min. The best course of action would be to:",
